#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:1
msgid "Mathematics and proofs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:4
msgid "An equation is useful when it makes a relation easier to inspect. Define every symbol before relying on it. State the units where they matter. The equation should support the explanation, not replace it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:8
msgid "Inline and display mathematics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:11
msgid "Use the ``math`` role for a short expression. Use the ``math`` directive for a display. Write the common LaTeX mathematics subset that both targets understand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:25
msgid "A side of length :math:`s` encloses area :math:`s^2`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:27
msgid "A = s^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:32
msgid "The relation is :eq:`area-square`. Guidedog gives a labelled equation a stable target. The HTML builder supplies MathJax markup. The PDF renderer translates the supported LaTeX subset to Typst mathematics. An unsupported PDF command is reported. Arbitrary LaTeX packages are not evaluated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:38
msgid "A small proof"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:41
msgid "Let :math:`U` be the bytes already charged to a budget. Let :math:`B` be its limit. Let :math:`n` be a requested allocation. Assume all three values are nonnegative and :math:`U \\le B`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:45
msgid "**Admission rule.** Admit the request exactly when"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:47
msgid "n \\le B - U."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:52
msgid "**Claim.** An admitted request preserves :math:`U + n \\le B`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:54
msgid "**Proof.** Add :math:`U` to both sides of :eq:`budget-admission`. The result is :math:`U+n \\le B`. The implementation tests the subtraction form. It does not first compute :math:`U+n`, which could overflow a machine integer. The proof and the arithmetic order agree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:59
msgid "**Boundary.** This accounts for charged host storage. It does not bound the separate Typst compiler's internal allocations. A proof is only as strong as the boundary it states."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:63
msgid "HTML setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:66
msgid "These projects bundle MathJax 3.2.2 with its SVG font data. The bundle is loaded only on pages containing mathematics. It makes these pages readable offline after a build. No diagram script or web font service is required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:71
msgid "For your own project, the default uses MathJax from a CDN. To serve a local copy, place it in ``_static`` and set:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/mathematics.rst:79
msgid "JavaScript is needed here to typeset web equations. Without it, their source remains visible. PDF equations do not need browser JavaScript. See the `MathJax component contract <https://docs.mathjax.org/en/v3.2/web/components/combined.html>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:1
msgid "How a project works"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:4
msgid "The project root is the directory containing ``conf.toml``. ``root_doc`` names the document that starts the reading order. Guidedog discovers source documents, reads them, resolves their relations, and publishes a complete output generation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:11
msgid "Pipeline traverses discovery, source plugins, parallel reading, reference resolution, rendering, HTML plugins, and atomic staging."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:11
msgid "Guidedog's end-to-end documentation compilation pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:35
msgid "Documents and resources"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:38
msgid "A source suffix selects a reader. RST and project Markdown share the project graph. Images and downloads are resources. Guidedog copies resources that the documents use. Theme assets are listed in ``html_static_path``. An include must remain within the source directory or the configured ``include_roots``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:43
msgid "Separate projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:46
msgid "Keep a manual and design records in separate projects. They have different readers and different reading orders. Each project has its own configuration, templates, cache, and output. The Guidedog repository uses ``docs/manual`` and ``docs/gds`` this way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:51
msgid "Incremental work"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:54
msgid "Guidedog records the inputs and lookups used by each output. A changed source is reread. A changed dependency invalidates its users. A changed template can affect every page. An unchanged document may be loaded from its saved object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:59
msgid "The cache is evidence about a previous build. It is not the source of truth. ``-E`` discards the saved environment for the next build. ``-a`` writes all outputs. They solve different problems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:63
msgid "\\text{reuse} = \\text{same inputs} \\land \\text{same lookups} \\land \\text{output exists}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:67
msgid "An explicit timestamp in a template is also an input to the rendered page. A page that prints the current minute can change even when its prose does not. Use ``SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`` when you need a fixed build time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:71
msgid "Publication"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:74
msgid "A build writes changes to a sibling staging directory. It completes that directory, flushes the files and records, and then switches generations. Readers see a complete previous or new publication. An interrupted commit is settled on the next build. The design and its proof obligations are in :doc:`../internals/publication`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:1
msgid "Typography for reading"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:4
msgid "A page should make its structure visible before the reader studies it. A strong title, a quiet navigation column, and a measured text column do most of the work. Color should mark a relation or an action. It should not carry the meaning alone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:8
msgid "The web page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:11
msgid "The custom layout has three regions: project navigation, the document, and the current page's outline. Small screens use a navigation drawer. The reading edition bundles Libertinus Serif and Libertinus Sans. Titles use a bold serif face. Navigation, labels, and controls use its sans companion. The compressed WOFF2 files are served with the site. There are no font-service requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:20
msgid "Navigation column on left, main document text column in center, local section outline on right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:20
msgid "Three-region reading layout for HTML documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:37
msgid "**Light is the default.** The theme control also offers dark and system modes. It remembers the choice on the same browser origin. System mode follows the operating system. Printed HTML uses light colors. Content and navigation remain usable without JavaScript. The theme control and enhanced search are progressive enhancements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:43
msgid "English prose can use paragraph-wide **Knuth-Plass line breaking** in the browser. A small template script measures the loaded font, builds boxes, spaces, and hyphenation penalties, and chooses breaks for the whole paragraph. Frank Liang's pattern method supplies English hyphenation candidates. The original TeX patterns and the libraries' licenses are bundled with the site."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:49
msgid "The enhancement preserves emphasis and the exact source text. It recalculates when the reading measure changes. Copying within one optimized paragraph gives the source text, without visual line divisions or added hyphens. Printing restores the ordinary paragraph so the print layout can paginate it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:54
msgid "Links, mathematics, code, interactive content, narrow measures, and other languages use native browser layout. So do paragraphs for which no suitably spaced solution exists. The site remains readable with JavaScript disabled. The fallback uses language-aware ``hyphens: auto`` and ``text-wrap: pretty``; those CSS properties alone are not a promise of Knuth-Plass optimization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:60
msgid "The PDF page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:63
msgid "The book template uses the same Libertinus family as the web edition. It sets justified paragraphs, optimized line breaking, and language-aware hyphenation. It also controls spacing, running heads, captions, code blocks, and table rules. The font, hyphenation cost, and paragraph measure work together. A forced line break is rarely the right repair for a loose paragraph."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:69
msgid "Typst optimizes the PDF paragraphs. The web enhancement optimizes eligible prose. They share the source, fonts, and visual hierarchy. Their line breaks can differ. See the `Typst text contract <https://typst.app/docs/reference/text/text/>`_ and the `browser hyphenation contract <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/hyphens>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:74
msgid "Change the design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:77
msgid "Edit ``_static/editorial.css`` for the documentation project's visual tokens. Edit ``_templates/layout.html`` for structure. Edit ``_templates/book.typ`` for the print layout. Keep reading order, focus indicators, labels, and sufficient contrast intact."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:82
msgid "Build both targets after a change. Inspect a page with code, a table, a diagram, and an equation. An attractive cover is not proof that the rest of the book works. Build your own design in :doc:`../tutorial/a-custom-theme`. The full template contract is in :doc:`../templates`. The complete API is in :doc:`../api/index` and in the same PDF book."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/writing.rst:1
msgid "Authoring documents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/writing.rst:4
msgid "Guidedog supports both reStructuredText (``.rst``) and MyST Markdown (``.md``). Documents are parsed into a shared semantic document tree, which is then used to generate both HTML websites and PDF books."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/writing.rst:8
msgid "Choose a source language"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/writing.rst:11
msgid "Use reStructuredText when you need full access to domains, complex tables, and extensible directives. Use MyST Markdown when you prefer Markdown syntax while retaining Sphinx-style directives and roles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/writing.rst:15
msgid "In a documentation project, Markdown documents are parsed using the MyST dialect. Single-document conversions with ``guidedog convert`` use standard CommonMark by default, unless the MyST reader is explicitly selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/writing.rst:19
msgid "Headings and structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/writing.rst:22
msgid "A document title identifies the page. Sections and subsections divide the text logically. In reStructuredText, headings use underline adornments (and optional matching overlines). The underline must be at least as long as the text."
msgstr ""

